From a677ae1b117fa2c58093df8e2bf81a2459c3ab4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 10:23:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] gatekeeper/devices: a device whose hostname dnsmasq gave to
 another one is no longer "Unknown device"

Field report (owner RT-BE96U, v3.1.1, 2026-09-07): the Gatekeeper page listed
device after device as "Unknown device", and an approved device stayed unknown.
Every Reaper name reader - gkd's seen table, the Gatekeeper and Devices CGIs -
took the self-reported name from /var/lib/misc/dnsmasq.leases. dnsmasq lets ONE
lease hold a given hostname at a time (lease.c lease_set_hostname: the newest
claimant wins and kill_name() blanks the other lease so a name maps to one
address in DNS). Four iPhones on that LAN all announce "iPhone", so at any moment
all but one read "*" in the lease file; dnsmasq logged "DHCPACK ... iPhone" for
the very MAC whose lease line was "*". A device that announces no hostname at all
(a printer, a Fire TV, a Lutron hub) never had a lease name; the stock network
map labels those by vendor, Gatekeeper showed nothing.

- shared/reaper_dhcpname.c (new, libshared): a per-MAC store of the hostname a
  client ANNOUNCED, /var/lib/misc/reaper_hostnames (tmpfs, tab-separated, one
  row per MAC newest last, 1024-row cap, tmp+rename under a flock). The name is
  client input: printable ASCII only, 32 bytes - the clamp the seen table already
  applies - and JSON-escaped again by every emitter. Reader caches on mtime/size.
- rc/services.c dnsmasq_script_main: on every add/old event record
  DNSMASQ_SUPPLIED_HOSTNAME (dnsmasq exports the raw option-12 value to the
  lease-change script, untouched by the uniqueness rule), falling back to the
  lease name; DNSMASQ_VENDOR_CLASS kept beside it. IPv4 events only.
- gkd: scan_supplied() after the lease scan fills a host the lease files could
  not give, so the syslog "new device" line and seen.tsv carry the name too.
- httpd: gk_display_name() = user name > lease/seen host > announced name >
  networkmap's own label (/jffs/nmp_cl_json.js "name": vendor class or OUI
  vendor when there is no hostname - what the stock map shows). Used by the
  Gatekeeper pending list and device table. The Devices page gets the same two
  fallbacks after its seen-table pass, and a vendor-labelled row says so: src
  "vendor", new token RDEV_96 in all 25 packs (lockstep 6894), chip on the page.

Gates: reaper_static_checks 11/11; shared, gkd, rc, httpd compile and link clean
in the warm tree (mk-% pass-through), no diagnostics in the edited regions.
Verify markers added (libshared path literal, rc env literal, gkd/httpd import).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
